      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The American Revolution and Paine's Role</strong><br>\nDuring the American Revolution, Paine served as a soldier and aide to General Nathanael Greene. He also continued to write, producing \"The American Crisis\" papers, which bolstered the spirits of the American soldiers and civilians. His opening line, \"These are the times that try men's souls,\" has become iconic.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Paine's Ideas on Government</strong><br>\nPaine was a pioneering advocate of free speech and democratic representation. He proposed forward-thinking ideas such as social security, universal basic income, and the abolition of slavery. His works emphasized the importance of a government that is representative and serves the welfare of all its people.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Rights of Man</strong><br>\nAfter the American Revolution, Paine returned to Europe and wrote \"The Rights of Man\" in defense of the French Revolution. He argued against monarchy and hereditary government, and he laid out a vision for a new type of government that would protect the rights of all individuals, not just the privileged.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
