          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Life and Imprisonment in France</strong><br>\nWhile in France, Paine was elected to the National Convention but was later imprisoned during the Reign of Terror for not endorsing the execution of Louis XVI. He spent nearly a year in jail before being released in 1794, thanks in part to the intervention of James Monroe, then the American minister to France.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The Age of Reason\"</strong><br>\nWhile in prison, Paine wrote \"The Age of Reason,\" in which he criticized organized religion and promoted deism. The work caused a decline in his popularity, as it was seen by many as an attack on Christianity. Nevertheless, it was an influential text on the development of freethinking.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Thomas Paine's Legacy</strong><br>\nThomas Paine died on June 8, 1809, in New York City. He left behind a legacy as a revolutionary who articulated the aspirations for freedom and equality. His writings continue to inspire people around the world in their fights for democratic rights and personal liberties. Despite facing social and political backlash during his life, his ideas have endured, influencing various social and political movements to this day.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
